//? Title: Longest Paths in a Directed Acyclic Graph

\toc

> Mathematics

The unrestricted simple longest-path problem is NP-hard.  In a DAG, a
topological order makes it dynamic programming:

$$
d(v)=\max_{(u,v)\in E}\{d(u)+w(u,v)\},\quad d(s)=0.
$$

Every predecessor has already been finalized, proving the recurrence exact.

> Pseudocode

```
topologically sort; reject a cycle
distance <- -infinity; distance[source] <- 0
for u in topological order:
  relax every u->v by maximum rather than minimum
```

Time is \(O(V+E)\).

> C\+\+ example

```{cpp, results="output", exports="both", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/algorithms/routing_batch.hpp>
#include <iostream>
int main(){auto r=datamunge::algorithms::dag_longest_paths(
4,{{0,1,2},{0,2,1},{1,3,3},{2,3,8}},0);std::cout<<r.distance[3]<<'\n';}
```
// result_begin:
// 9
// result_end

The best route is \(0\to2\to3\), of length 9.
